          "source": "kantei-funka",
          "score": 0.0306,
          "cos": 0.709
        }
      ],
      "a": "Volcanic ash can cause skin inflammation upon contact [1]. It may lead to pain, swelling, or allow bacteria to enter through scratches [1]. Volcanic ash is made of small, hard, and sharp pieces of rock, which can injure the eyes and lungs [1]. Because volcanic ash is very fine, it can enter deep into the lungs along with air, causing increased coughing or shortness of breath [2]. It can also cause nasal discharge, phlegm, and pain in the nose or throat [2]. People with asthma or bronchitis should be cautious as they may experience severe coughing, chest tightness, or wheezing [2]. Individuals with serious heart conditions should also be careful [2]. If volcanic ash gets in your eyes, it can cause a gritty sensation, itching, pain, and redness; watery discharge or tears may occur [2]. If the surface of the eye is scratched by volcanic ash, conjunctivitis can occur, leading to stinging or sensitivity to light [2].",
      "cited": [
        1,
        2
      ],
